<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Li Jiqian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">963–1004</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Li Jiqian (963–1004) was a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanguts">Tanguts</a> of the Ordos region. When his kinsman surrendered the family's hereditary territories to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> in 982, he refused, fled into the steppe and waged a long war against the Song, allying with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a>. He captured Lingzhou in 1002 but died of wounds in 1004, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-deming">Li Deming</a> and grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-yuanhao">Li Yuanhao</a> built the Western Xia state on his gains.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/li-jiqian.jpg" alt="Li Jiqian" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BabelStone · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:XiXia_Tomb_1B.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
